      "rank": 1,
      "ballot_id": "B029",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Hidetoshi Katori", "Jun Ye"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks have transformed precision timekeeping and enabled sensitive tests of fundamental physics. This is a major advance in atomic physics and measurement.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye developed complementary leading clock approaches and drove the field toward unprecedented precision. The award should recognize both lines of work.",
        "maturity": "The methods have been refined and independently reproduced over many years, with sustained improvements in accuracy and applications.",
        "uncertainties": ["The relative priority and contributions of other groups in the transition from proposals to practical clocks would need careful review."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B012",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Yakir Aharonov", "Michael Berry"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov–Bohm and Berry phases reveal that quantum phase has observable consequences beyond local forces and energy levels. Their reach across quantum physics makes this a profound contribution.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov’s prediction of the electromagnetic phase effect and Berry’s general account of geometric phase are distinct, complementary contributions.",
        "maturity": "The effects have extensive experimental confirmation and are now standard concepts across many areas of physics.",
        "uncertainties": ["The broad later development of geometric phases involved many contributors, so the boundaries of the credited discovery need to be stated precisely."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alexander A. Belavin", "Alexander B. Zamolodchikov", "Alexander M. Polyakov"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Conformal field theory provided an exact and remarkably powerful framework for two-dimensional critical phenomena, with influence extending well beyond its original setting.",
        "attribution": "The credited authors jointly established the core framework and its application to critical systems; joint recognition is appropriate.",
        "maturity": "The results have been foundational for decades and are deeply integrated into statistical physics and quantum field theory.",
        "uncertainties": ["The theoretical achievement is clear, though its fit to a prize traditionally centered on experimentally grounded discoveries may be debated."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B018",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Immanuel Bloch", "Markus Greiner", "Peter Zoller"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical-lattice experiments made controlled quantum many-body systems accessible and opened a new way to investigate strongly correlated matter.",
        "attribution": "Zoller supplied influential theoretical ideas for quantum simulation, while Bloch and Greiner led landmark experimental realizations. This selection captures both theory and experiment.",
        "maturity": "The platform has developed through many independent experiments and now supports a broad research program.",
        "uncertainties": ["The credited work spans proposals, early demonstrations, and later capabilities; the committee should define which advances anchor the prize."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B022",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Carlton M. Caves", "H. Jeff Kimble", "Roman Schnabel"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Squeezed light turns quantum fluctuations into a controllable resource for precision measurement, a significant advance in quantum optics with direct relevance to gravitational-wave detection.",
        "attribution": "Caves established the theoretical measurement framework, Kimble helped develop experimental squeezed-light methods, and Schnabel advanced their use in precision instruments.",
        "maturity": "The effect is well established experimentally and has moved from laboratory demonstrations into major measurement systems.",
        "uncertainties": ["The development involved several experimental groups, and the distinct contributions of the proposed laureates should be assessed against that broader history."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Albrecht Karle", "Christian Spiering", "Francis Halzen"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "IceCube established high-energy neutrinos as a new means of observing the universe, opening a distinct channel for astrophysics.",
        "attribution": "Halzen was central to the detector concept and its realization; Karle and Spiering made substantial contributions to the experiment and its development.",
        "maturity": "The observatory has operated for years and produced a growing body of results, supporting the significance of the discovery.",
        "uncertainties": ["The discovery and detector were the work of a large collaboration, making individual credit difficult to delimit."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B016",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alfred Y. Cho", "Federico Capasso", "Jérôme Faist"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum cascade laser introduced a new kind of laser based on engineered semiconductor transitions and enabled compact sources across important infrared bands.",
        "attribution": "Capasso and colleagues established the device concept and its first realization; Cho’s contributions to semiconductor epitaxy were important to making the structure possible.",
        "maturity": "The device principle has been repeatedly demonstrated, developed across wavelengths, and widely adopted.",
        "uncertainties": ["The division between the original design, first demonstration, and enabling materials growth should be examined in assigning credit."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B024",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alexandre Blais", "Robert J. Schoelkopf", "Steven M. Girvin"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Circuit quantum electrodynamics made strong interactions between microwave photons and artificial atoms experimentally accessible, creating a central platform for quantum optics and quantum information.",
        "attribution": "Blais developed key theoretical ideas, while Schoelkopf and Girvin helped establish and explain the experimental circuit platform.",
        "maturity": "The basic physics has been demonstrated broadly and the platform has supported sustained advances in control and measurement.",
        "uncertainties": ["The field continues to evolve rapidly, and it remains to be judged which results constitute the lasting discovery rather than later applications."]
      }
    }
  ]
